From 5e1453680f2ee20b7666a8416287e91cd133a36c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 15:25:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] lifecycle guard: a benign session alias no longer
 makes every call argument an executed script
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Problem: on the MacBook gateway (core 7c16f596ed, 2026-11-13 06:44 and 06:49 PST) the terminal guard
refused
  ssh ... ubuntu@192.0.2.10 'bash -ls' <<'EOF'
  python3 - <<'PY'
  ... os.path.exists(os.path.expanduser("~/.hermes/kanban.db")) ...
  PY
  EOF
with "`~/.hermes/kanban.db` is a SQLite database open in this gateway process". The same
`python3 - <<'PY'` body run locally was allowed.

Five whys
- Why 1: the walk treated the db path as an *executed* candidate. It appears only as a call
  argument (`expanduser("…")`), but a call-argument candidate counts as executed whenever
  `budget.aliases_possible` is true (cron/lifecycle_guard.py `_walk_candidates`).
  Evidence: scan_gateway_lifecycle(repro, session_aliases=X) on origin/main a3d2eed676 returns
  (False, None) for X=frozenset() and (True, "...SQLite database open...") for X={"ll"} or None.
- Why 2: `aliases_possible` was `session_aliases is None or bool(session_aliases)`. Any alias in
  the backend's login snapshot (a stock `alias ll='ls -l'`) made every `NAME (` in the command a
  possible keyword-led subshell, even when no alias was named NAME.
  Evidence: c3bbf092e9 (review 5888336995 nit 2), in 7c16f596ed.
- Why 3: the local control passes because the body there is masked (`python3 -` is a trusted data
  consumer). Nested in `bash -ls`, the body stays visible by design (a login shell sources startup
  files that may rewrite it, review 5882059941), so its call arguments reach the walk. So neither
  card hypothesis holds: `python3 -` is recognized as a data consumer, and the inner heredoc is
  parsed. The alias rule is what flips the mention to "executed".
- Root (happened): the session-alias guard tested "does the shell hold any alias?" when bash can
  only turn `NAME (` into a subshell if a word before the `(` is itself an alias.
- Root (not caught): #1671's and c3bbf092e9's tests call scan_gateway_lifecycle with the default
  session_aliases=frozenset(). The real gateway passes the snapshot's names. The shape corpus
  checks only one direction (bash ran it, main refuses, head must refuse). Its "benign" `ll`
  snapshot never had a call-argument mention of an unscannable file, so no false refusal could show.
- Recurrence: same false-refusal class as t_01a1d0ea6db6c158 (#1671) and its follow-up
  t_01a1d45302555c0d. #1671 fixed call-argument mentions; c3bbf092e9 then reopened them for every
  gateway whose login snapshot defines an alias.

Fix
- `_LifecycleScanBudget.alias_names` replaces `aliases_possible`: the known session alias names,
  or None when any name may be an alias (unknown snapshot, non-str names, or walked text that may
  define an alias, which is sticky as before).
- `_iter_segment_contexts(alias_names=…)`: a `(` whose opener segment holds any known alias word
  is never a call argument, so it stays in the executed view. Any word counts, not only the
  command word, which covers blank-ending alias chains.
- `_walk_candidates`: a call-argument candidate is executed only when alias names are unknown.
  With no session aliases nothing changes. With unknown names every call argument is still
  executed.
- Boundary: alias effects are taken to stay within the segment that names the alias. An alias
  whose value leaves a quote or heredoc unbalanced re-lexes the rest of the line; shlex never models that, here or elsewhere in the guard.

Proof
- RED (origin/main code, new tests): 5 failed. These are the exact repro under {"ll"} and under
  the Ubuntu stock alias set, the end-to-end LocalEnvironment snapshot with `alias ll='ls -l'`
  through gateway_lifecycle_block, and the controls' discriminating `{"zz"}` mention assertion.
  GREEN: 18/18.
- Fail-closed controls (main refuses each, head refuses each): `t ( bash <live db> )` and
  `ll x ( bash <live db> )` with session {"ll","t"}; unknown names (None); malformed names
  frozenset({1}); an in-text `alias T=' '`. A real-bash test shows `alias ll='time '` makes
  `ll ( echo ran )` run, and that without the alias it is a syntax error.
- Existing guard tests unchanged and green: 23 files importing lifecycle_guard / shell_heredoc /
  terminal_tool_guards, 1598 passed.
- Full shape corpus (`heredoc_shape_corpus.py --full`): 20350 cases, bash ran 5192,
  violations=0.
- Mutants (6), each killed by the touched test files: drop the alias-led opener check; never treat
  names as unknown; make note_aliases a no-op; drop the non-str name check; treat None as known;
  stop passing names to segment discovery.
- Replay over 5469 real terminal commands from worker-8's state.db (old main vs head, with
  kanban.db and state.db held open as a gateway holds them):
  - no session aliases or unknown names: 0 verdicts change.
  - {"ll"} or the stock set: 1 change, the card's repro.
  - the Ubuntu set with `ls`: 5 changes. All 5 are the same false-refusal class: a db path only
    inside `expanduser("…")`/`connect(…)` in a heredoc body.
  - 0 commands went from allowed to refused.

Card: t_01a1d565699c975b
---
 cron/lifecycle_guard.py                       | 61 ++++++++----
 .../test_lifecycle_guard_session_aliases.py   | 98 +++++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 138 insertions(+), 21 deletions(-)

diff --git a/cron/lifecycle_guard.py b/cron/lifecycle_guard.py
index 1dbb0bc6f4..89a0d74cc0 100644
--- a/cron/lifecycle_guard.py
+++ b/cron/lifecycle_guard.py
@@ -497,7 +497,7 @@ class _LifecycleScanBudget:
     cloud placeholder) so the caller can tell the model the real reason (#113944)."""
 
     __slots__ = (
-        "aliases_possible",
+        "alias_names",
         "bytes_remaining",
         "lines_remaining",
         "mask_heredocs",
@@ -522,18 +522,24 @@ class _LifecycleScanBudget:
         # Message-only: newlines a caller added when joining the root text (cron's empty-prompt
         # join). Charged like any line, never reported as the text's own; spent on the first charge.
         self.separator_lines = separator_lines
-        # Sticky for the whole walk: any walked text (the command, an eval payload, a sourced
-        # script) that may define an alias makes `NAME ( … )` possibly a keyword-led subshell. So do
-        # aliases the command's shell already holds (*session_aliases*, see `scan_gateway_lifecycle`).
-        self.aliases_possible = session_aliases is None or bool(session_aliases)
+        # Alias names that may expand in walked text: the ones the command's shell already holds
+        # (*session_aliases*, see `scan_gateway_lifecycle`), or ``None`` when any name may be an
+        # alias: unknown session names, or (sticky for the whole walk) any walked text (the
+        # command, an eval payload, a sourced script) that may define one. An alias can make
+        # `NAME ( … )` a keyword-led subshell; only a `(` whose opener holds one of these names
+        # (or any `(`, when ``None``) may be one (t_01a1d565699c975b: a lone `alias ll='ls -l'`
+        # once made `expanduser("~/.hermes/kanban.db")` an executed script).
+        self.alias_names: Optional[frozenset[str]] = (
+            session_aliases if isinstance(session_aliases, frozenset)
+            and all(isinstance(name, str) for name in session_aliases) else None)
         # False when a session alias may shadow a heredoc consumer, wrapper or shell name
         # (`alias python3=bash`): then no heredoc body is provably inert, so none is masked.
         self.mask_heredocs = not _session_aliases_shadow_consumers(session_aliases)
 
     def note_aliases(self, text: str) -> None:
         """Record (sticky) that walked *text* may define or enable an alias."""
-        if not self.aliases_possible:
-            self.aliases_possible = _may_define_alias(text)
+        if self.alias_names is not None and _may_define_alias(text):
+            self.alias_names = None
 
     def charge_text(self, text: str) -> bool:
         """Charge *text* before tokenization; False when it does not fit (``overrun`` says why)."""
@@ -838,14 +844,20 @@ def _session_aliases_shadow_consumers(session_aliases: Optional[frozenset[str]])
                for name in session_aliases)
 
 
-def _iter_segment_contexts(command: str) -> Iterator[tuple[list[str], bool, list[str]]]:
+def _iter_segment_contexts(
+    command: str, alias_names: frozenset[str] = frozenset(),
+) -> Iterator[tuple[list[str], bool, list[str]]]:
     """``(segment, call_argument, piped_files)``: whether the segment is the argument list of a
     call (the segment right after a ``(`` that follows an ordinary word), and the files a ``cat``
     pipes into its stdin (``cat f.sh | bash``), through groups, compound commands and process
-    substitution too (``{ cat f; } | bash``, ``cat f | if …; fi``, ``bash <(cat f)``)."""
+    substitution too (``{ cat f; } | bash``, ``cat f | if …; fi``, ``bash <(cat f)``). A ``(``
+    whose opener holds any word in *alias_names* is never a call: the alias may expand to a
+    keyword prefix (``alias t='time '``)."""
+    def opens_call(segment: list[str]) -> bool:
+        return _opens_call_arguments(segment) and alias_names.isdisjoint(segment)
     for tokens in _iter_command_token_lists(command):
         yield from iter_line_segment_contexts(
-            tokens, cat_operands=_cat_operands, opens_call_arguments=_opens_call_arguments,
+            tokens, cat_operands=_cat_operands, opens_call_arguments=opens_call,
             peel_reserved_words=_peel_reserved_words)
 
 
@@ -1359,14 +1371,16 @@ def _references_at(
 
 def _iter_referenced_shell_scripts(
     command: str, *, cwd: Optional[str] = None, call_arguments: bool = False,
+    alias_names: frozenset[str] = frozenset(),
 ) -> Iterator[Path]:
     """Yield scripts executed directly or through a POSIX shell. Each segment is read at the
     original token AND at the peeled wrapper target, both with and without leading reserved words
     (`time`, `!`, `then`, `{`, …) — additive on purpose: peeling must never REMOVE
     a reference (a local ``./timeout`` is a script, not the coreutils wrapper). With
     *call_arguments* the same discovery runs over call-argument segments instead
-    (``expanduser("~/x")``): paths there are interpreter string literals, i.e. mentions."""
-    for segment, is_call_argument, piped in _iter_segment_contexts(command):
+    (``expanduser("~/x")``): paths there are interpreter string literals, i.e. mentions.
+    A ``(`` led by one of *alias_names* opens no call (see ``_iter_segment_contexts``)."""
+    for segment, is_call_argument, piped in _iter_segment_contexts(command, alias_names):
         if is_call_argument != call_arguments:
             continue
         for view in _command_segment_views(segment):
@@ -1703,31 +1717,35 @@ def _walk_candidates(
     trip them. Executed candidates come first so a mention never starves a real script's budget. A
     path in interpreter call syntax (`expanduser("~/x")`, `connect('/d/x.db')`) is a string
     literal the shell never runs: a mention too, read and scanned but never a fail-closed verdict,
-    unless an alias may turn its `NAME (` into a subshell (`alias T=' '; T ( bash big.sh )`). That
-    is decided lazily, when the entry is reached: the executed candidates before it (a sourced
-    script may define the alias) have been walked by then.
+    unless an alias may turn its `NAME (` into a subshell (`alias T=' '; T ( bash big.sh )`). A
+    `(` led by a known session alias name is never a call (the executed view). Otherwise it is
+    decided lazily, when the entry is reached: the executed candidates before it (a sourced
+    script may define an alias, making every name a possible alias) have been walked by then.
 
     A file run as ``python3 <file>`` (masked or not) comes last, as ``python_source``: regex-scanned
     only, never walked as shell, and never failed closed. A file the scan cannot read (oversized, a
     stream such as ``/dev/stdin``, a device) ran before this scan existed, so only a lifecycle
     command actually found in it blocks.
     """
+    names = budget.alias_names or frozenset()
     entries = [
-        (path, executed, False) for path in _iter_referenced_shell_scripts(walk_command, cwd=cwd)
+        (path, executed, False)
+        for path in _iter_referenced_shell_scripts(walk_command, cwd=cwd, alias_names=names)
     ]
     entries += [
         (path, executed, True)
-        for path in _iter_referenced_shell_scripts(walk_command, cwd=cwd, call_arguments=True)
+        for path in _iter_referenced_shell_scripts(
+            walk_command, cwd=cwd, call_arguments=True, alias_names=names)
     ]
     if walk_command != command:
         entries += [
             (path, False, False)
             for call_arguments in (False, True)
             for path in _iter_referenced_shell_scripts(
-                command, cwd=cwd, call_arguments=call_arguments)
+                command, cwd=cwd, call_arguments=call_arguments, alias_names=names)
         ]
     for path, candidate_executed, call_argument in entries:
-        yield path, candidate_executed and (budget.aliases_possible or not call_argument), False
+        yield path, candidate_executed and (budget.alias_names is None or not call_argument), False
     # Python candidates are never executed (fail-closed) entries, so the unmasked text, a superset
     # of the masked view, finds them all.
     for path in _iter_python_interpreter_scripts(command, cwd=cwd):
@@ -1858,8 +1876,9 @@ def scan_gateway_lifecycle(
     *session_aliases* are the alias names the shell running *command* already holds before its
     first line, outside the walked text: the terminal backend sources a login snapshot holding
     ``alias -p`` and ``shopt -s expand_aliases`` (``tools/environments/base_session_env.py``).
-    ``None`` means it may hold aliases with unknown names. Any session alias makes ``NAME ( … )``
-    possibly a keyword-led subshell (``alias t='time '``), and one that may rename a heredoc
+    ``None`` means it may hold aliases with unknown names. A session alias name in the words before
+    a ``(`` makes ``NAME ( … )`` possibly a keyword-led subshell (``alias t='time '``); unknown names
+    make every such ``(`` one. A session alias that may rename a heredoc
     consumer, wrapper or shell (``alias python3=bash``) leaves every heredoc body visible.
 
     Total by construction: never raises. Direct scans are pure string ops; the referenced-script
diff --git a/tests/cron/test_lifecycle_guard_session_aliases.py b/tests/cron/test_lifecycle_guard_session_aliases.py
index e3ca323b69..c192b07fdb 100644
--- a/tests/cron/test_lifecycle_guard_session_aliases.py
+++ b/tests/cron/test_lifecycle_guard_session_aliases.py
@@ -5,6 +5,7 @@ every command runs with them. The guard must hear about them from the backend.""
 import json
 import subprocess
 from types import SimpleNamespace
+from typing import Any
 from unittest.mock import patch
 
 import pytest
@@ -81,6 +82,103 @@ def test_guard_reads_backend_hook_on_the_class_only():
     assert _session_aliases_for_guard(SimpleNamespace(cwd="/")) == frozenset()
 
 
+# --- t_01a1d565699c975b: a benign session alias must not make every call argument executed -------
+# The card's repro, verbatim: refused on the MacBook gateway (its login snapshot defines aliases
+# such as `ll`) as "`~/.hermes/kanban.db` is a SQLite database open in this gateway process".
+_CARD_REPRO = (
+    "ssh -o BatchMode=yes -o ConnectTimeout=15 ubuntu@192.0.2.10 'bash -ls' <<'EOF'\n"
+    "python3 - <<'PY'\n"
+    "import os\n"
+    "print(\"nested ssh python heredoc ok:\", "
+    "os.path.exists(os.path.expanduser(\"~/.hermes/kanban.db\")))\n"
+    "PY\n"
+    "EOF"
+)
+# Ubuntu's stock ~/.bashrc aliases, and a lone `ll` (the corpus's "benign" snapshot).
+_STOCK_ALIASES = frozenset({"ls", "grep", "fgrep", "egrep", "ll", "la", "l", "alert"})
+
+
+@pytest.fixture
+def live_kanban_db(tmp_path, monkeypatch):
+    """`~/.hermes/kanban.db` held open (tracked) in this process, as the gateway holds its own."""
+    from hermes_cli.sqlite_safe_read import connect_tracked
+
+    home = tmp_path / "home"
+    (home / ".hermes").mkdir(parents=True)
+    monkeypatch.setenv("HOME", str(home))
+    conn = connect_tracked(home / ".hermes" / "kanban.db")
+    conn.execute("CREATE TABLE t (x)")
+    conn.commit()
+    try:
+        yield home / ".hermes" / "kanban.db"
+    finally:
+        conn.close()
+
+
+@pytest.mark.parametrize("names", [frozenset(), frozenset({"ll"}), _STOCK_ALIASES])
+def test_card_repro_is_not_refused_under_benign_session_aliases(tmp_path, live_kanban_db, names):
+    """The db path is only an interpreter call argument (`expanduser("…")`): no session alias
+    names `expanduser`, so bash cannot turn that `(` into a subshell. A mention, never a refusal."""
+    assert scan_gateway_lifecycle(_CARD_REPRO, cwd=str(tmp_path), session_aliases=names) == (False, None)
+
+
+@pytest.mark.parametrize("command", [
+    "t ( bash {db} )",  # the alias is the opener: may be `alias t='time '`
+    "ll x ( bash {db} )",  # any session alias word in the opener counts (blank-ending chains)
+])
+def test_session_led_call_still_fails_closed(tmp_path, live_kanban_db, command):
+    """Controls: a `(` led by a word the session holds as an alias stays executed, and a text that
+    may define an alias, or unknown names, keep every call argument executed (main refuses all).
+    No path here may contain the word "alias": that alone marks the text as alias-defining."""
+    assert "alias" not in str(tmp_path).lower()
+    text = command.format(db=live_kanban_db)
+    # The same words with no session alias in the opener: a call argument, i.e. a mention.
+    assert scan_gateway_lifecycle(text, cwd=str(tmp_path), session_aliases=frozenset({"zz"})) == (
+        False, None)
+    # `frozenset({1})`: a malformed backend answer (non-str names) is unknown names, not none.
+    cases: list[tuple[str, Any]] = [
+        (text, frozenset({"ll", "t"})), (text, frozenset({1})), (_CARD_REPRO, None),
+        ("shopt -s expand_aliases; alias T=' '\n" + _CARD_REPRO, frozenset({"ll"}))]
+    for checked, aliases in cases:
+        unsafe, refusal = scan_gateway_lifecycle(checked, cwd=str(tmp_path), session_aliases=aliases)
+        assert unsafe is True and refusal is not None and "SQLite" in refusal, aliases
+
+
+def test_alias_led_session_subshell_runs_in_real_bash(tmp_path):
+    """Execution proof for the control above: with `ll` a blank-ending snapshot alias, bash runs
+    `ll ( … )` as a subshell, so a session alias in the opener must keep the walk fail-closed."""
+    out = subprocess.run(["/bin/bash", "--norc", "--noprofile", "-s"],
+                         input="shopt -s expand_aliases\nalias ll='time '\nll ( echo ran )\n",
+                         text=True, capture_output=True, timeout=10, check=False).stdout
+    assert out.strip() == "ran"
+    # Without an alias the same words are a syntax error: nothing runs.
+    plain = subprocess.run(["/bin/bash", "--norc", "--noprofile", "-s"], input="ll ( echo ran )\n",
+                           text=True, capture_output=True, timeout=10, check=False)
+    assert "ran" not in plain.stdout and plain.returncode != 0
+
+
+def test_local_backend_benign_snapshot_alias_allows_card_repro(tmp_path, live_kanban_db, monkeypatch):
+    """End to end through the terminal guard with a real snapshot holding `alias ll='ls -l'`."""
+    from tools.environments.local import LocalEnvironment
+
+    init_file = tmp_path / "custom-init.sh"
+    init_file.write_text("alias ll='ls -l'\n")
+    with patch("tools.environments.local._read_terminal_shell_init_config",
+               return_value=([str(init_file)], False)):
+        env = LocalEnvironment(cwd=str(tmp_path), timeout=15)
+        try:
+            names = env.lifecycle_guard_session_aliases()
+            assert names is not None and "ll" in names
+            monkeypatch.setattr("tools.process_registry._is_supervised_gateway_process",
+                                lambda: True)
+            result = gateway_lifecycle_block(
+                command=_CARD_REPRO, env=env, env_type="local",
+                cwd=str(tmp_path), workdir=str(tmp_path), session_key="benign-alias-test")
+        finally:
+            env.cleanup()
+    assert result is None
+
+
 def test_local_backend_reports_snapshot_aliases_and_terminal_guard_refuses(tmp_path, monkeypatch):
     """End to end: a login-file alias reaches the snapshot, the backend reports it, and the
     terminal guard refuses the reviewer's repro (it allowed it before the fix)."""
